I think the changes will be limited to bug fixes and a small grab-bag
of characteristically oddball patches.

Ascending in NetHack gives a greater sense of accomplishment than
ascending in Slash'EM, even though the latter is harder. Allow me to
explain.

Slash'EM changes frequently, and responds to user demands by adding
features like firearms, giant shops and powergaming items. There is a
Slash'EM community that has a hand in developing the game, and as such
playing it is rather like playing your own game. A /lot/ of the things
we do in NetHack are truly pointless and arbitrary, but we do them and
enjoy it because that's the challenge handed down to us. "Now descend
through 25 levels of mazes!" NetHack says. Slash'EM listens to users
and shortens Gehennom. It's almost like cheating. If you don't like a
feature of the game, send a patch to the Slash'EM DevTeam, and it's
gone in the next version! You can savescum, add patches, remove
nasties, or any number of things which, on paper, might even make the
game better from an objective point of view, but it won't be NetHack
any more.

Completing a challenge is always more satisfying if it's specified by
someone else. Running a mile because you /have/ to might not be more
fun than doing it because you /want/ to, but the sense of achievement
is incomparable. Aiming for 20, then deciding that 19 pushups is enough
is a cop-out; solving an equation you just made up is fruitless; doing
your own crosswords is no fun at all. These things are no fun because
you are essentially playing with yourself. You aren't interacting with
an unknown.

Put simply, NetHack has Authority. It's a known, monolithic task. Its
details are decided by a faceless Other, who has laid down a fixed task
and issued a challenge. NetHack's authority is derived from its
stability, and its stability is derived from its long release cycle.
It's nice to have new features (providing they are organic outgrowths
of the same core game), but biannual point-releases would be an end to
the NetHack experience as we know it.

